        "summary": "PyCrypto is an abandoned cryptographic library whose final release (2.6.1) shipped over 12 years ago and carries four known vulnerabilities, including a critical buffer overflow and weak key generation. Despite its still-large install base, it should not be used for any new or existing security-sensitive code.",
        "reasons": [
          "The latest and only relevant version, 2.6.1, has 4 known vulnerabilities: a critical buffer overflow (GHSA-cq27-v7xp-c356), a high-severity weak key generation issue (GHSA-6528-wvf6-f6qg), and two further issues including a heap-based overflow in block_template.c that allows arbitrary code execution.",
          "There has been no release in 4,493 days (over 12 years) and zero releases in the last 12 months, so none of these vulnerabilities will ever be patched — the project is effectively unmaintained.",
          "Weak ElGamal key parameters (PYSEC-2018-97) mean that even correctly written code using the library can silently produce breakable keys, which is disqualifying for a cryptography package.",
          "The 2,054,006 weekly downloads show the package is still widely installed, but that reflects legacy lockfiles and tutorials rather than health; adoption does not fix unpatched critical CVEs.",
          "No GitHub repository is linked in the metadata, so there is no visible upstream to report issues to or track fixes from."
        ],
